  [1m-kernel_profile[0m[=true|[4mfalse[0m]
    Add top level event and stall ports required by kernel profiling
  [1m-module_auto_prefix[0m[=false|[4mtrue[0m]
    Use top name as prefix for all internal (non-top) entity/module names
  [1m-module_prefix[0m <string> 
    Specify a prefix for all entity/module names
  [1m-mult_keep_attribute[0m[=true|[4mfalse[0m]
    Enable keep attribute
  [1m-register_all_io[0m[=true|[4mfalse[0m]
    Use a register by default for all I/O signals
  [1m-register_reset_num[0m <int:[4m0[0m> 
    Number of registers to add to reset signal
  [1m-reset[0m <none | state | all | [4mcontrol[0m>
    Variables initialized in the C code are always initialized to the same value in the RTL and hence in the bit-stream.
    This initialization however is only performed at power-on and not repeated when a reset is applied to the design.
    The setting applied with the -reset option determines how registers/memories are reset.
      [1mnone[0m : no reset is added to the design
      [1mcontrol[0m : reset control registers, such as those used in state machines and to generate IO protocol signals (default)
      [1mstate[0m : reset control registers and registers/memories derived from static/global variables in the source code. Any static/global variable initialized in the source code is reset to its initialized value
      [1mall[0m : reset all registers and memories. Any static/global variable initialized in the source code is reset to its initialized value
  [1m-reset_async[0m[=true|[4mfalse[0m]
    This option causes all registers to use an asynchronous reset. If this option is not specified a synchronous reset is
    used.
  [1m-reset_level[0m <low | [4mhigh[0m>
    This option allows the polarity of the reset signal to be either active low or active high

